## Why This Is Asked
Tests memory ownership across CGO: who frees what, and how to convert C strings safely.

## Key Concepts
- CGO memory ownership and free responsibilities
- Converting C strings to Go with C.GoString
- Handling arrays allocated in C and freeing them
- Error mapping from C to Go errors

## Code Example
```c
// error.h
#ifndef ERROR_H
#define ERROR_H

typedef struct {
    int code;
    char* msg;
} ErrorInfo;

ErrorInfo* get_errors(int* count);

#endif
```

```c
// error.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "error.h"

ErrorInfo* get_errors(int* count) {
    *count = 2;
    ErrorInfo* errors = malloc(sizeof(ErrorInfo) * (*count));

    errors[0].code = 404;
    errors[0].msg = strdup("Not Found");

    errors[1].code = 500;
    errors[1].msg = strdup("Internal Server Error");

    return errors;
}
```

```go
// error.go
package main

/*
#cgo CFLAGS: -I.
#cgo LDFLAGS: -L. -lerror
#include "error.h"
#include <stdlib.h>
*/
import "C"

import (
	"fmt"
	"unsafe"
)

type CGError struct {
	Code    int
	Message string
}

func Errors() ([]CGError, error) {
	var count C.int
	errorPtr := C.get_errors(&count)
	if errorPtr == nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("failed to get errors")
	}

	defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(errorPtr))

	result := make([]CGError, int(count))
	slice := (*[1 << 28]C.ErrorInfo)(unsafe.Pointer(errorPtr))[:count:count]

	for i := 0; i < int(count); i++ {
		result[i].Code = int(slice[i].code)
		result[i].Message = C.GoString(slice[i].msg)
		C.free(unsafe.Pointer(slice[i].msg))
	}

	return result, nil
}

func main() {
	errors, err := Errors()
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Printf("Error: %v\n", err)
		return
	}

	for _, err := range errors {
		fmt.Printf("Code: %d, Message: %s\n", err.Code, err.Message)
	}
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle error messages that are not UTF-8?
- What changes if get_errors returns NULL on error vs empty list?

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces a practical CGOA integration pattern not covered by prior samples: preallocated buffers, error propagation across the C-Go boundary, and concurrent calls.

## Key Concepts

- CGO memory management across FFI
- errno-based error reporting
- thread-safety and Go concurrency with goroutines
- proper freeing of C resources

## Code Example

```c
// header
#ifndef FORMAT_USER_H
#define FORMAT_USER_H
#include <stddef.h>
int format_user(char* out, size_t max, const char* name, int age);
#endif
```

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include "format_user.h"
int format_user(char* out, size_t max, const char* name, int age){
  if(!out || max==0) { errno=EINVAL; return -1; }
  int needed = snprintf(out, max, "User:%s,Age:%d", name, age);
  if(needed >= (int)max) { errno=ERANGE; return -1; }
  return needed;
}
```

```go
// Go binding
package main
/*
#include "format_user.h"
*/
import "C"
import (
  "fmt"
  "unsafe"
)
func FormatUser(name string, age int) (string, error) {
  buf := make([]byte, 256)
  cname := C.CString(name)
  defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(cname))
  n := C.format_user((*C.char)(unsafe.Pointer(&buf[0])), C.size_t(len(buf)), cname, C.int(age))
  if n < 0 {
     return "", fmt.Errorf(C.GoString(C.strerror(C.errno)))
  }
  return string(buf[:n]), nil
}
```

